  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` couldn't handle prices with thousands separators. The regex didn't match a price like `\"2,450.50\"`, so `m` was `null` and `m[1]` threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. `checkout` doesn't catch that, so the server returned a 500.\n\nThe log has 37 such errors. The DEBUG payload line just before each one shows the price was always `1,299.00`, `2,450.50` or `1,005.99`. Orders with no comma in the price succeeded, which is why the failures looked intermittent.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- The regex now accepts properly grouped thousands (`1,299.00`, `1,000,000`), and the commas are stripped before parsing.\n- Malformed input like `abc` or `12,34` now throws the documented `Error('Invalid amount: …')` instead of a `TypeError`. This was already the documented behaviour, but the code didn't do it.\n\nI did not change `src/server.js`, so any invalid price still returns a 500 rather than a 400.\n\n**Regression tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- Comma-formatted amounts parse correctly.\n- Malformed amounts throw `Invalid amount`.\n- `checkout` handles `\"2,450.50\"`.\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 of 7 tests, and the two parsing tests fail against the old code.",
